# THREAT MODELING RULES
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

## STRIDE Analysis Format:
When performing STRIDE analysis, always use this structure:

```
### STRIDE Threat Analysis

#### 🎭 Spoofing (Identity)
- Threat: [Description]
- Impact: [High/Medium/Low]
- Mitigation: [Control]

#### ✏️ Tampering (Data Integrity)
- Threat: [Description]
- Impact: [High/Medium/Low]
- Mitigation: [Control]

#### 🚫 Repudiation (Non-repudiation)
- Threat: [Description]
- Impact: [High/Medium/Low]
- Mitigation: [Control]

#### 📤 Information Disclosure (Confidentiality)
- Threat: [Description]
- Impact: [High/Medium/Low]
- Mitigation: [Control]

#### 💥 Denial of Service (Availability)
- Threat: [Description]
- Impact: [High/Medium/Low]
- Mitigation: [Control]

#### ⬆️ Elevation of Privilege (Authorization)
- Threat: [Description]
- Impact: [High/Medium/Low]
- Mitigation: [Control]
```

## Data Flow Diagram Requirements:
Always include in DFDs:
- External entities (users, systems)
- Processes (applications, services)
- Data stores (databases, files)
- Data flows with labels
- Trust boundaries (clearly marked)

## Attack Tree Format:
When creating attack trees:
- Root node: Primary attack goal
- Child nodes: Sub-goals or attack methods
- Leaf nodes: Specific techniques
- Mark nodes as AND/OR
- Include likelihood and impact ratings

